<!-- OOP SPOTLIGHT -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">OOP Spotlight: Overriding vs Tweaking Data</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="concept-explanation" style="background: #f8f9fa; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;">
        <p><strong>Attribute override:</strong> In this level, Brute and Scout change values like <code>health</code> and <code>accuracy</code> after calling <code>super()</code>. Same methods, different data.</p>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Method override:</strong> You can also change behaviour by overriding a method. This is optional and not required by the validator.</p>
        <div style="margin-top: 1rem; background: #fff3cd; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 4px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>Optional extension:</strong> Override <code>get_status()</code> in Brute or Scout to include the class name (e.g., "Brute Alex: 120/120 HP"). Keep the same method name and return type.
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">This makes polymorphism visible: the engine calls the same method, but each class can respond differently.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- STRUCTURE GUIDE -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Structure Guide</h2>
    </div>
    
    <p class="section-intro">Click each section to expand. You'll create TWO new classes in a new file.</p>
    
    <!-- 1. Import Player -->
    <details class="skeleton-block" open>
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">1</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">Import the Parent Class</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-line code">IMPORT Player FROM student.player</div>
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                You need access to Player so your new classes can inherit from it.
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
    
    <!-- 2. Brute Class -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">2</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">Create the Brute Class</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-line code">CLASS Brute INHERITS FROM Player:</div>
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                <strong>The Tank Archetype:</strong> Create a constructor that:
                <ul>
                    <li>Calls the parent constructor first (pass the name)</li>
                    <li>Then overrides: health = 120, max_health = 120</li>
                    <li>armour = 3</li>
                    <li>accuracy = 70</li>
                </ul>
                <em>Hint: Use super() to call the parent constructor, then set the attributes you want to change.</em>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
    
    <!-- 3. Scout Class -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">3</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">Create the Scout Class</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-line code">CLASS Scout INHERITS FROM Player:</div>
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                <strong>The Precision Archetype:</strong> Create a constructor that:
                <ul>
                    <li>Calls the parent constructor first (pass the name)</li>
                    <li>Then overrides: health = 80, max_health = 80</li>
                    <li>armour = 1</li>
                    <li>accuracy = 95</li>
                </ul>
                <em>Hint: Same pattern as Brute — call parent first, then customise.</em>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>

    <!-- 4. describe_specialty -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">4</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">Add describe_specialty()</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-line code">METHOD describe_specialty() → string:</div>
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                Return a short description of what makes the class unique.
                <ul>
                    <li>Brute: high health and armour</li>
                    <li>Scout: high accuracy and mobility</li>
                </ul>
                This shows method overriding: same method name, different output.
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
    
    <!-- 5. Understanding Inheritance -->
    <details class="skeleton-block">
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">5</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">How Inheritance Works</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="skeleton-desc">
                Both Brute and Scout <strong>inherit</strong> from Player, meaning they get all Player methods (get_status, take_damage, heal) for free.
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">By calling the parent constructor and then overriding specific attributes, each class becomes a specialised version of Player with its own stats.</p>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">By overriding <code>describe_specialty()</code>, each class can also change behaviour while keeping the same method name.</p>
                <div class="skeleton-line code" style="margin-top: 1rem;">brute = Brute("Tank")<br>scout = Scout("Ghost")</div>
                <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Both work with any code that expects a Player — that's the power of inheritance.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
</section>

